---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { publicationPath } from '../lib/site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Sentence Receptacle — Constraint Grammar of Completed Intelligibility"
  description="A conceptual prospect for using the sentence as a concrete observational window onto completed intelligibility."
  sourceStatus="Conceptual prospect · not an implemented analyser"
>
  <article class="page receptacle-page">
    <header class="receptacle-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Conceptual prospect</p>
      <h1>Sentence Receptacle</h1>
      <p class="lede">A sentence is small enough to hold in the hand and rich enough to show a whole act of intelligibility becoming complete.</p>
      <div class="origin-riddle" aria-label="A question before language">
        <p class="origin-riddle__eyebrow">Before the sentence</p>
        <p class="origin-riddle__question">Why can a pattern mean something before we have words for what it means?</p>
        <p class="origin-riddle__answer">A firework unfolds and we follow it. A kaleidoscope turns and something resolves. A mandala, a tessellation, a tiling, a repeating rhythm: each can become intelligible before it becomes verbal.</p>
        <p class="origin-riddle__answer">They seem to strike a note between <strong>word-fullness and wordlessness</strong>—enough form to be grasped, enough openness that the grasp has not yet been exhausted by explanation.</p>
        <p class="origin-riddle__answer">And something more peculiar happens: we do not entirely choose the moment of recognition. Completion arrives. A pattern settles, releases, clicks, lands. We find ourselves at a point of realisation before we have decided to be there.</p>
        <p class="origin-riddle__answer">The runtime exists to give that situation room to <strong>resume its own lawful unfolding</strong>: not to tell the viewer what to realise, but to make possible the encounter in which intelligibility is found involuntarily.</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">The idea</p>
      <h2>Treat the sentence as a window.</h2>
      <p>The sentence is not being proposed here as the ontology of Constraint Grammar. It is a privileged observational case: compact, inspectable, repeatable, and already central to the intellectual history of the project.</p>
      <p>A future Sentence Receptacle could take one sentence seriously enough to ask what had to become complete for that sentence to become intelligible as the sentence it is.</p>
      <p>In that practical sense, the sentence can function as a <strong>unit of currency of intelligibility</strong>: a small exchangeable object through which the larger grammar can be seen, tested, compared, and explained.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">Possible operation</p>
      <h2>One sentence in. A structured reading out.</h2>
      <div class="steps">
        <div><span>01</span><h3>Receive the sentence</h3><p>Preserve the sentence exactly as given. The analysis would begin from a concrete completed form rather than replacing it with a paraphrase.</p></div>
        <div><span>02</span><h3>Ask what was open</h3><p>Identify the unresolved demand or opening pressure against which the sentence becomes intelligible as a completion.</p></div>
        <div><span>03</span><h3>Read the change</h3><p>Ask which Transformation Pattern best describes what became different.</p></div>
        <div><span>04</span><h3>Read what made it enough</h3><p>Ask how completion becomes sufficient: Focal, Distributed, or Release.</p></div>
        <div><span>05</span><h3>Read what remains</h3><p>Ask how the completed result persists: Standing, Bearing, or Efficacy.</p></div>
        <div><span>06</span><h3>Show the whole relation</h3><p>Use the resulting coordinate as a disciplined way to explain the sentence, compare nearby possibilities, and illuminate the grammar without pretending the analysis is uniquely forced when the evidence does not support that claim.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="window-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why this could matter</p>
      <h2>The sentence can make the whole theory visible at human scale.</h2>
      <p>A fifty-four-position architecture is easier to understand when a reader can watch one ordinary sentence acquire structure. The Receptacle is therefore proposed as an explanatory instrument first: a way to let the theory reveal itself through a familiar object.</p>
      <p>A mature implementation could let readers compare alternative readings, hold one coordinate constant while changing another, inspect neighboring forms, trace the difference between a completed form and its formation history, and see why intelligibility can be analyzed before truth or correctness is judged.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="substrate-destination">
      <p class="eyebrow">The wordless place</p>
      <h2>A place of realisation.</h2>
      <p>The Sentence Receptacle is one way of looking back from language. A sentence gives us a finished, human-scale object through which completion can be inspected. But the riddle points further back: toward the kind of lawful pattern we already recognise in an unfolding firework, a kaleidoscope, a mandala, a tessellation, or a tiled field—structure that can become graspable before it has been converted into prose.</p>
      <p><strong>Theorem Zero — Canonical Substrate v1.0</strong> is the current expression of that wordless origin-point. Its canonical substrate defines a locked infinite triangular / barycentric field. Finite activations select from that field; overlays and rendering express those selections without acquiring authority over the substrate itself.</p>
      <p>The runtime is not chiefly an illustration of a theory already stated elsewhere. It is an opportunity for the lawful situation to resume itself in front of the viewer. Emergence, relation, repetition, completion, release: the viewer is not first told what these mean. Instead, the conditions are restored in which some form of completion may be found involuntarily.</p>
      <p>That is the wager of the runtime: that intelligibility can occur as an event of realisation before it becomes an act of description. We recognise enoughness before we explain enoughness. We meet completion before we possess its vocabulary.</p>
      <p>This is not a claim that the runtime has proven the metaphysical origin of language, thought, or reality. It is a disciplined philosophical placement inside this project: before the sentence, before the explanatory grammar, before the words, there is a proposed lawful field in which something may already become intelligible.</p>
      <a class="theorem-zero-link" href={publicationPath('theorem-zero/')}>
        <span><strong>Enter Theorem Zero</strong><small>Canonical Substrate v1.0 · the wordless runtime</small></span>
        <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </section>

    <section class="boundary-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">Boundary</p>
      <h2>This is a conceptual doorway, not a claimed capability.</h2>
      <p>No sentence-analysis engine is being claimed here. The Receptacle does not classify arbitrary sentences, prove psychological reality, establish unique coordinates, or demonstrate empirical validity. Those would be separate engineering and research achievements.</p>
      <p>The purpose of this page is to make one prospect explicit without pretending it already exists: <strong>one sentence as a window into the completed act of intelligibility, with a wordless place of realisation waiting behind it.</strong></p>
    </section>

    <footer class="receptacle-footer">
      <a href={publicationPath('wordless/')}>← Wordless</a>
      <a href={publicationPath('history/')}>Why sentences mattered historically →</a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .receptacle-page { max-width: 860px; }
  .receptacle-hero { padding: 3rem 0 4rem; }
  .receptacle-hero h1 { max-width: 9ch; font-size: clamp(3.4rem, 9vw, 7.5rem); line-height: .92; letter-spacing: -.055em; margin: .35rem 0 1.4rem; }
  .receptacle-hero .lede { max-width: 700px; font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2.4vw, 1.65rem); }
  .origin-riddle { margin-top:2.35rem; padding:1.25rem 1.35rem 1.35rem; border-left:3px solid #9e978c; background:linear-gradient(90deg,rgba(245,241,233,.78),rgba(255,253,248,0)); }
  .origin-riddle p { margin:0; }
  .origin-riddle__eyebrow { font:700 .66rem/1.3 ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif; letter-spacing:.12em; text-transform:uppercase; color:#777168; }
  .origin-riddle__question { max-width:25ch; margin-top:.55rem !important; font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif; font-size:clamp(1.35rem,3vw,2rem); line-height:1.15; font-style:italic; }
  .origin-riddle__answer { max-width:64ch; margin-top:.78rem !important; color:#676159; font-size:.92rem; }
  section { padding: 3rem 0; border-top: 1px solid #ddd7cb; }
  section h2 { max-width: 16ch; font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.4rem); line-height:1; margin:.35rem 0 1.5rem; letter-spacing:-.035em; }
  section > p:not(.eyebrow) { max-width: 68ch; font-size: 1.05rem; }
  .steps { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr)); gap:1rem; margin-top:2rem; }
  .steps > div { padding:1.35rem 1.4rem; border:1px solid #ddd7cb; border-radius:16px; background:#fffdf8; }
  .steps span { font:700 .68rem/1 ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif; letter-spacing:.1em; color:#68645d; }
  .steps h3 { margin:.55rem 0 .65rem; font-size:1.15rem; }
  .steps p { margin:0; color:#514d47; }
  .window-section { position:relative; }
  .window-section:after { content:'“'; position:absolute; right:0; top:1.5rem; font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif; font-size:8rem; line-height:1; color:#e8e2d7; pointer-events:none; }
  .substrate-destination { position:relative; overflow:hidden; margin:2rem -1.5rem 0; padding:3rem 1.5rem; border:1px solid #263b42; border-radius:20px; background:radial-gradient(circle at 88% 20%,rgba(100,255,210,.12),transparent 32%),linear-gradient(145deg,#0a1219,#101f28); color:#eef7f3; }
  .substrate-destination:after { content:''; position:absolute; width:15rem; height:15rem; right:-7rem; bottom:-8rem; border:1px solid rgba(100,255,210,.22); border-radius:50%; box-shadow:0 0 0 2.7rem rgba(100,255,210,.035),0 0 0 5.4rem rgba(143,185,255,.025); pointer-events:none; }
  .substrate-destination .eyebrow { color:#a7c8c0; }
  .substrate-destination h2 { max-width:17ch; }
  .substrate-destination > p:not(.eyebrow) { color:#c9d8d4; }
  .theorem-zero-link { position:relative; z-index:1; display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:space-between; gap:1rem; margin-top:2rem; padding:1rem 1.1rem; border:1px solid rgba(100,255,210,.34); border-radius:14px; color:#f4fff9; text-decoration:none; background:rgba(100,255,210,.055); transition:transform .15s ease,background .15s ease,border-color .15s ease; }
  .theorem-zero-link:hover { transform:translateY(-2px); background:rgba(100,255,210,.09); border-color:rgba(100,255,210,.58); }
  .theorem-zero-link:focus-visible { outline:3px solid #64ffd2; outline-offset:3px; }
  .theorem-zero-link strong { display:block; font-size:1rem; }
  .theorem-zero-link small { display:block; margin-top:.2rem; color:#9fb9b2; font:600 .72rem/1.4 ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif; letter-spacing:.03em; }
  .theorem-zero-link > span:last-child { font-size:1.45rem; }
  .boundary-section { background:#f5f1e9; margin-left:-1.5rem; margin-right:-1.5rem; padding-left:1.5rem; padding-right:1.5rem; border-radius:18px; }
  .receptacle-footer { display:flex; justify-content:space-between; gap:1rem; padding:2.5rem 0 1rem; font-weight:700; }
  .receptacle-footer a { text-underline-offset:.2em; }
  @media(max-width:700px){
    .receptacle-hero { padding-top:2rem; }
    .steps { grid-template-columns:1fr; }
    .window-section:after { display:none; }
    .receptacle-footer { flex-direction:column; }
  }
  @media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.theorem-zero-link{transition:none}}
</style>
